
  <sn><md># Complete Guide to AppMan on Ubuntu Linux

A practical installation, management, and troubleshooting manual for AppImage applications

This manual covers AppMan from the beginning: what it is, how it works, how to install it on Ubuntu, how to install and manage AppImages, how to change installation directories, how to move existing applications to another drive, and how to uninstall applications completely.

The commands are written primarily for Ubuntu, but most of the instructions also apply to other Debian-based Linux distributions.

## 1. What is AppMan?

[GitHub - ivan-hc/AppMan: AppImage Package Manager. "AppMan" is a redistribution of "AM" Application Manager, that does not require root privileges to install and manage apps: same code, same apps... two different behavior.](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/b222w1pLwDyZypWQ-ZnzQuEkAgY1sbC7sKphI8kYEiXq-nC9C-TESaV64mSdbtr97dgfAztVAxMg0LQtuH4xdIvGMSDbpRJLfUb0rz3HB1wXNqjhxqZc2E0aeCzur9tC-l-EMVYqrhD9H6-cEyfsvuW-dsTinqx9apsUj2aGvPY?purpose=inline)

AppMan

Open-source • Bash-based • Rootless • Command-line

Official project: [AppMan on GitHub](https://github.com/ivan-hc/AppMan?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

AppMan is a Linux application manager developed by Ivan-HC. It helps you install, update, integrate, and uninstall AppImage applications and other portable Linux applications.

Think of it as a package manager similar to APT, but focused on portable applications.

For example:

| Package manager | Main purpose                                |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| APT             | Installs `.deb` packages                    |
| Snap            | Installs Snap packages                      |
| Flatpak         | Installs Flatpak applications               |
| AppMan          | Manages AppImages and portable applications |

### What makes AppMan different?

Without AppMan, installing an AppImage generally involves:

1. Downloading an AppImage manually.
2. Moving it to a directory.
3. Giving it executable permission.
4. Creating a desktop launcher manually.
5. Finding and downloading updates manually.
6. Removing the application and its launcher manually.

AppMan automates much of this process.

Its main features include:

- Installing applications from its application database.
- Installing AppImage-only applications.
- Creating application launchers.
- Making applications available from the terminal.
- Updating installed applications.
- Uninstalling applications.
- Installing applications without root privileges.
- Choosing a custom installation directory.
- Relocating applications to another directory or drive.
- Supporting external AppImage sources.
- Optional application sandboxing.

An important distinction: AppMan does not turn every application into an AppImage. It can also manage certain portable applications distributed as archives or other supported formats. 

