## 2. AppMan vs AM: What is the difference?

AppMan and AM are closely related projects. They use the same underlying application-management engine but operate differently.

| Feature                | AppMan                   | AM                                    |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------- |
| Installation scope     | User-level               | System-level and user-level           |
| Root privileges        | Not normally required    | Required for system-wide installation |
| Application directory  | User-selected            | Usually `/opt`                        |
| Command                | `appman`                 | `am`                                  |
| Application management | Local applications       | Local and system applications         |
| Suitable for           | Individual desktop users | Administrators and advanced users     |

### Which one should you use?

For a regular Ubuntu desktop, AppMan is a good choice.

You can install and manage applications without running every installation command with `sudo`.

For example:

```
appman -ia google-chrome
```

You do not need:

```
sudo appman -ia google-chrome
```

In fact, avoid running AppMan with `sudo`. It is designed to manage applications in your user environment.

## 3. Understanding AppImage before using AppMan

Before installing applications, it helps to understand what an AppImage actually is.

### What is an AppImage?

An AppImage is a portable application format for Linux.

It usually contains an application and many of the libraries and resources required to run it.

For example:

```
GoogleChrome.AppImage
```

You can download this file, make it executable, and run it without installing a traditional `.deb` package.

### How does an AppImage work?

[openSUSE Conference 2017: Your desktop application as a cross-distribution AppImage - Speaker Deck](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/p99ZR8xOuZsjnnTOrRQRj5DBc71wIMLLbBrJlXTkHp5aqeUW1TeD8gOSv9N85lDZfE1xYKglFpeDmMOiEbtxpKwFw7-dtyvbmpsXlJbTzjZECas4z_GOXGQdHj05f9f_bvyqqR81q_KzjXZzzaoRR0hEh7Z7bYc0fs9HWg9Hw8Y?purpose=inline)

[speakerdeck.com](https://speakerdeck.com/probonopd/opensuse-conference-2017-your-desktop-application-as-a-cross-distribution-appimage?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

An AppImage generally contains:

1. Runtime: Handles launching and mounting the application.
2. SquashFS filesystem: Contains the application files.
3. Application binaries: The actual executable programs.
4. Libraries and resources: Dependencies, icons, themes and other resources.

When you launch an AppImage:

1. Linux executes its runtime.
2. The runtime accesses the embedded filesystem.
3. The application files become accessible.
4. The main application executable starts.

AppImages are generally self-contained, but they are not guaranteed to work on every Linux distribution. Some require additional system libraries or FUSE support.

### AppImage vs traditional installation

| Feature            | Traditional DEB        | AppImage                          |
| ------------------ | ---------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Installation       | Package installation   | Usually just download and execute |
| Root permission    | Usually required       | Usually not required              |
| System integration | Package manager        | Optional integration              |
| Updates            | APT repositories       | Application-specific              |
| Removal            | APT uninstall          | Delete file or use manager        |
| Portability        | Distribution-dependent | Generally portable                |

### AppImage types

There are two commonly encountered generations:

- Type 1: Older AppImage format.
- Type 2: Newer and more widely used AppImage format.

Some older Type 2 applications require `libfuse2` to run.

On Ubuntu, if you encounter a FUSE-related error, check your Ubuntu release and install the appropriate compatibility package:

```
sudo apt update
sudo apt install libfuse2
```

Do not install this package automatically unless it is needed. Some newer Ubuntu releases use a differently named compatibility package.

## 4. Installing AppMan on Ubuntu

There are two ways to install AppMan.

### Method 1: Official installer (recommended)

This is the easiest and recommended method.

Step 1. Install required dependencies

Open your terminal:

```
sudo apt update
sudo apt install curl wget grep sed coreutils
```

Step 2. Download the official installer

```
wget -q https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ivan-hc/AM/main/AM-INSTALLER
```

Step 3. Give it executable permission

```
chmod +x AM-INSTALLER
```

Step 4. Run the installer

```
./AM-INSTALLER
```

The installer will present two choices:

```
1. Install AM
2. Install AppMan
```

Choose:

```
2
```

This installs AppMan in your user environment.

Step 5. Remove the installer file

```
rm AM-INSTALLER
```

Installation complete

### Method 2. Install AppMan using a single command

If you prefer a one-line installation:

```
wget -q https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ivan-hc/AM/main/AM-INSTALLER \
&amp;&amp; chmod +x AM-INSTALLER \
&amp;&amp; ./AM-INSTALLER \
&amp;&amp; rm AM-INSTALLER
```

Choose option 2 when prompted.

### Where is AppMan installed?

The installer normally places the executable here:

```
~/.local/bin/appman
```

Check its location:

```
which appman
```

Expected output:

```
/home/emon/.local/bin/appman
```

You can also use:

```
command -v appman
```

Check its version:

```
appman -v
```

Check available commands:

```
appman -h
```

### If the terminal cannot find AppMan

Check whether the directory is in your PATH:

```
echo "$PATH"
```

If `~/.local/bin` is missing, add it to your Bash configuration:

```
echo 'export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"' &gt;&gt; ~/.bashrc
```

Reload the configuration:

```
source ~/.bashrc
```

Now try:

```
appman -h
```

Official installation instructions: [AppMan installation documentation](https://github.com/ivan-hc/AppMan?utm_source=chatgpt.com).

## 5. First-time AppMan configuration

When you run AppMan for the first time, it asks where you want to store your applications.

This is one of AppMan's most useful features.

For example, you might choose:

```
/home/emon/Applications
```

Or:

```
/home/emon/AppImages
```

Or a directory on another drive:

```
/mnt/data/Applications
```

AppMan uses this directory as its main application storage location.

### Recommended directory structure

I recommend keeping portable applications separate from your normal documents.

For example:

```
/home/emon/
│
├── Applications/
│   ├── GoogleChrome/
│   ├── Firefox/
│   ├── SublimeText/
│   ├── Cursor/
│   └── OtherApplications/
│
├── .local/
│   ├── bin/
│   └── share/
│       └── applications/
│
└── .config/
    └── appman/
        └── appman-config
```

The `Applications` directory stores the application files, while `.local/bin` and `.local/share/applications` contain launch commands and desktop launchers.

### Where is the configuration stored?

AppMan's configuration file is:

```
~/.config/appman/appman-config
```

Read it:

```
cat ~/.config/appman/appman-config
```

You can also inspect the directory:

```
ls -lah ~/.config/appman/
```

Important: Do not simply edit the configuration file to change the application directory. Changing it changes where AppMan looks for applications; it does not automatically move existing installations. Use the relocation procedure described later. 

