## 6. Finding applications available in AppMan

AppMan maintains an online database of applications with installation scripts.

You can search for applications rather than downloading AppImages manually.

### Search for an application

For example, search for Google Chrome:

```
appman -q google-chrome
```

Search for Firefox:

```
appman -q firefox
```

Search for Sublime Text:

```
appman -q sublime-text
```

The exact application identifier matters. Use the name shown in the search results.

### Display available applications

```
appman -l
```

### Get information about an application

```
appman -a google-chrome
```

This can display application information and its source details.

### Find the complete application database

You can browse the online application catalog:

[Portable Linux Apps – Application Database](https://portable-linux-apps.github.io/apps/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

This is useful when you do not know the exact application identifier.

## 7. Installing Linux applications with AppMan

AppMan supports several installation methods.

### Method 1. Install a normal application

The general syntax is:

```
appman -i APPLICATION_NAME
```

For example:

```
appman -i firefox
```

This installs an application using its available installation script.

### Method 2. Install only AppImage applications

If you specifically want AppImages and do not want AppMan to select other supported portable formats, use:

```
appman -ia APPLICATION_NAME
```

For example:

```
appman -ia google-chrome
```

Another example:

```
appman -ia obs-studio
```

The `-ia` option means install AppImage.

This is particularly useful if your goal is to maintain a collection of AppImage applications.

### Method 3. Install an AppImage with sandboxing

AppMan also supports optional sandboxing.

```
appman -ia --sandbox APPLICATION_NAME
```

For example:

```
appman -ia --sandbox firefox
```

The shorter dedicated option is:

```
appman -ias firefox
```

Sandboxing can help restrict an application's access to parts of your system, but the actual protection depends on the sandbox configuration and application requirements.

### Method 4. Install an AppImage from an external GitHub repository

Sometimes an application is not included in AppMan's main database.

AppMan provides an external GitHub installation option:

```
appman -e USER/REPOSITORY APPLICATION_NAME
```

For example, the syntax is:

```
appman -e developer/project myapplication
```

The repository must provide an AppImage that AppMan can identify and manage.

### Method 5. Install a manually downloaded AppImage

If you already have an AppImage file, you can integrate it into the desktop menu using AppMan's launcher integration:

```
appman --launcher /path/to/application.AppImage
```

For example:

```
appman --launcher ~/Downloads/MyApplication.AppImage
```

This is different from installing an application from the database: it integrates an existing AppImage rather than necessarily creating a fully managed AppMan installation.

You can also use the `-e` option for supported external GitHub projects when you want AppMan to manage the application and its updates.

