## 9. Listing installed applications

AppMan provides commands to see which applications are installed.

### List installed applications

```
appman -f
```

This displays installed programs and their sizes.

### Sort applications by name

```
appman -f --byname
```

### Display only the number of installed applications

```
appman -f --less
```

### Find the installation directory of an application

You can inspect the application directory:

```
ls -lah ~/Applications/
```

For example:

```
ls -lah ~/Applications/google-chrome/
```

Find the command-line launcher:

```
which google-chrome
```

Find its desktop entry:

```
ls ~/.local/share/applications/
```

### Find the actual application executable

For example:

```
find ~/Applications/google-chrome -maxdepth 2 -type f
```

This helps you understand where AppMan has placed the application's files.

## 10. Updating installed applications

One of the biggest advantages of AppMan is application update management.

You do not need to manually visit every application's website to download updates.

### Update all installed applications

```
appman -u
```

### Update a specific application

```
appman -u google-chrome
```

### Update applications with debugging information

```
appman -u --debug
```

This is useful when an application fails to update and you want to investigate the problem.

### Update AppMan itself

```
appman -s
```

This synchronizes AppMan with its latest script version.

### Check the current version

```
appman -v
```

Note: AppMan's updater depends on the application-specific installation script. Not every upstream application necessarily provides the same update mechanism. 

