## 11. Uninstalling applications with AppMan

You can uninstall an application without manually deleting all its files.

### Method 1. Normal uninstall

```
appman -r APPLICATION_NAME
```

For example:

```
appman -r google-chrome
```

AppMan will ask you to confirm the removal.

### Method 2. Uninstall without confirmation

```
appman -R APPLICATION_NAME
```

For example:

```
appman -R google-chrome
```

Be careful with uppercase `-R`: it removes the application without asking for confirmation.

### Uninstall multiple applications

```
appman -r firefox google-chrome obs-studio
```

You can also use the non-interactive form:

```
appman -R firefox google-chrome obs-studio
```

### What happens when you uninstall an application?

AppMan normally removes its managed application directory and associated integration files, including:

- Application files.
- Update helper.
- Removal script.
- Application command launcher.
- Desktop launcher.
- Associated application icons.

Application-specific settings and user data may not be removed. These can remain in your home directory.

For example:

```
~/.config/google-chrome/
~/.cache/google-chrome/
~/.local/share/google-chrome/
```

These directories may contain browser profiles, cache, extensions, and user settings.

Important: If you want to preserve your browser profile, back it up before uninstalling. Do not blindly delete the entire `.config` or `.local/share` directories.

## 12. Changing the installation directory

This is one of the most important AppMan features.

Suppose your current installation directory is:

```
/home/emon/Applications
```

But you want to use:

```
/mnt/data/Applications
```

There are two different situations:

1. You have not installed any applications yet.
2. You already have applications installed and want to move them.

Let's examine both.

### Situation A. Changing the directory before installing applications

If AppMan has not been configured yet, you can set the location before its first run.

For example:

```
appman_location="$HOME/Applications" appman
```

Or:

```
appman_location="/mnt/data/Applications" appman
```

This works only when AppMan's configuration file does not already exist.

AppMan will use the specified directory when creating its configuration.

### Situation B. Changing the directory after installing applications

Suppose you already have these applications:

```
/home/emon/Applications/
├── google-chrome/
├── firefox/
├── sublime-text/
└── obs-studio/
```

You want to move everything to:

```
/mnt/data/Applications/
```

Do not simply change `appman-config`.

Doing so changes the location AppMan expects to use for future operations, but does not relocate your existing applications.

Instead, AppMan provides a dedicated relocation command.

### Use the official relocation command

```
appman relocate
```

The relocation procedure removes and reinstalls your local/AppMan applications at a new location.

Follow its prompts and choose the new directory.

Recommended relocation method

This is safer than manually moving directories because AppMan needs to maintain its application-management structure and integrations.

Official documentation: [Changing the destination path](https://github.com/ivan-hc/AM?utm_source=chatgpt.com#how-to-set-the-path-to-local-apps). 

